---
mm: 1.2
kind: module
name: rp2040-ram-mcu
module: platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
use: mm.mcu
file: mcu.cppm
option: buildable-host no
---

# platform.rp2040_ram.mcu

rp2040-ram's implementation of mm.mcu. It drives the RP2040 register map
directly and names no third-party library, so it stays core.

No application names it. The rp2040-ram board manifest binds mm.mcu to it, and
an executable whose closure reaches mm.mcu is linked against it because of the
platform it is building for. In any other lane it is an unavailable skipped
target, so nothing can link a registration for hardware that is not there.

It is a sibling of the board rather than a folder inside it because a kind:board
manifest has no folder: key: boards name their own sources, and a module is a
manifest of its own. The module name spells the board with an underscore because
a C++ module name component is an identifier; the board's platform-provider
declaration is the authoritative mapping between the two, and no tool derives
either spelling from the other.
